          %title Contents
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/files/HamburgAnnouncement.jpg", :width => "850"}/
          %p
            These notes (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Schreiber_pQFT20211103.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
            , 323 pages) mean to give an expository but rigorous introduction to the basic concepts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+quantum+field+theory"} relativistic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theories"} perturbative quantum field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theories"} Lagrangian field theories
            – such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+gravity"} perturbative quantum gravity
            appearing in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %blockquote
            %p
              This is one chapter of
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
            %p
              Previous chapters:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+sets"} smooth sets
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+supergeometry"} supergeometry
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Geometry"} Geometry
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Spacetime"} Spacetime
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Fields"} Fields
              %li
                %a{:href => "#FieldVariations"} Field variations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Lagrangians"} Lagrangians
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Symmetries"} Symmetries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Observables"} Observables
              %li
                %a{:href => "#PhaseSpace"} Phase space
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Propagators"} Propagators
              %li
                %a{:href => "#GaugeSymmetries"} Gauge symmetries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#ReducedPhaseSpace"} Reduced phase space
              %li
                %a{:href => "#GaugeFixing"} Gauge fixing
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Quantization"} Quantization
              %li
                %a{:href => "#FreeQuantumFields"} Free quantum fields
              %li
                %a{:href => "#QuantumObservables"} Interacting quantum fields
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Renormalization"} Renormalization
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            For broad introduction of the idea of the topic of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} there
            and see
          %ul
            %li
              PhysicsForums-Insights:
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://www.physicsforums.com/insights/paqft-idea-references/"} Introduction to Perturbative Quantum Field Theory
          %p
            Here, first we consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            (or rather
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-quantum+field+theory"} pre-quantum field theory
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
          %p
            We aim to give a
            %em
              fully
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local
            discussion, where all structures arise on the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
            over the
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#FieldVariations"} below
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression"} transgress
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+field+histories"} spaces of field histories
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            (discussed
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Observables"} further below
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Higher+Prequantum+Geometry"} Higher Prequantum Geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Khavkine"} Igor Khavkine
          %p
            In full beauty these concepts are extremely general and powerful; but the aim here is to give a first precise idea of the subject, not a fully general account. Therefore we concentrate on the special case where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MinkowskiSpacetime"}
            below), where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldsAndFieldBundles"}
            below) is an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+vector+bundle"} trivial vector bundle
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TrivialVectorBundleAsAFieldBundle"}
            below) and hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalLagrangianDensityOnSecondOrderJetBundleOfTrivialVectorBundleOverMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            below) is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+variational+field+theory"} globally defined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            we consider just the special case that the
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+parameter"} gauge parameter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+vector+bundle"} trivial vector bundle
            and we concentrate on the case that the gauge symmetries are “closed irreducible” (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GaugeParametersClosed"}
            below). But we aim to organize all concepts such that the
            %em structure
            of their generalization to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetime"} curved spacetime
            and non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundles"} field bundles
            is immediate.
          %p
            This comparatively simple setup already subsumes what is considered in traditional texts on the subject; it captures the established
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV+formalism"} BRST-BV quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
            coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetime"} on curved spacetimes
            – which is the state of the art. Further generalization, necessary for the discussion of global topological effects, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton"} instanton
            configurations of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} homotopical algebraic quantum field theory
          %p
            Alongside the theory we develop the concrete examples of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+scalar+field"} real scalar field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            and the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
          %p#RunningExamples
            %strong running examples
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation+of+motion"} equation of motion
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+scalar+field"} real scalar field
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  expl.
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RealScalarFieldBundle"}
