                      \\right)
                      \\,.
              %p
                Now, as in the first point above, here the first component is uniquely fixed to be the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+morphism"} zero morphism
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mn 0
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi odd
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n \overset{0}{\to} S_{odd}
            #DiracFieldSupergeometric.num_remark
              %h6#remark_3 Remark
              %p
                %strong
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometric
                  nature of the
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
              %p
                Proposition
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiracSpaceOfFieldHistories"}
                how two basic facts about the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    On the one hand a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+history"} field history
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Ψ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
                    of the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
                    is
                    %em not
                    an ordinary section of an ordinary
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ψ
                            %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi^A
                    anti-commute with each other, which is not the case for ordinary functions, and this is crucial for the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
                    of the Dirac field to be well defined, we come to this below in example
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianDensityForDiracField"}
                %li
                  %p
                    On the other hand a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+history"} field history
                    of the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
                    is supposed to be a
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
                    of a
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
              %p
                Therefore prop.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiracSpaceOfFieldHistories"}
                serves to shows how, even though a Dirac field is not defined to be an ordinary section of an ordinary vector bundle, it is nevertheless encoded by such an ordinary section: One says that this ordinary section is a “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superfield"}> superfield
                \-component” of the Dirac field, the one linear in a Grassmann variable
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi θ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
            %p
              This concludes our discussion of the concept of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
              itself. In the
              %a{:href => "#FieldVariations"} following chapter
              we consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variational calculus
              of fields.
          %div
            %h2#FieldVariations Field variations
            %p In this chapter we discuss these topics:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "##JetBundles"} Jet bundles
              %li
                %p
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "#DifferentialOperators"} Differential operators
              %li
                %p
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "#VariationaCalculusAndVariationalBicomplex"} Variational calculus and the Variational bicomplex
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
              as in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldsAndFieldBundles"}
              above, then we know what
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
              of quantities the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+histories"} field histories
              assign to a given spacetime point (a given
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/event"} event
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhood"} infinitesimal neighbourhood
              of any spacetime point (remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#JetBundleInTermsOfSyntheticDifferentialGeometry"}
              below). This means equivalently that the realized field histories should be those that satisfy a given
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation"} equation
              between the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+derivatives"} partial derivatives
              of the field history at any spacetime point. This is called the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation+of+motion"} equation of motion
              of the field theory (def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EulerLagrangeEquationsOnTrivialVectorFieldBundleOverMinkowskiSpacetime"}
              below).
            %p
              In order to formalize this, it is useful to first collect all the possible partial derivatives that a field history may have at any given point into one big space of “field derivatives at spacetime points”. This collection is called the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#JetBundleOfTrivialVectorBundleOverMinkowskiSpacetime"}
              below.
            %p
              Moving around in this space means to change the possible value of fields and their derivatives, hence to
              %em vary
              the fields. Accordingly
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variational calculus
              of fields is just
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+calculus"} differential calculus
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#VariationalBicomplexOnSecondOrderJetBundleOverTrivialVectorBundleOverMinkowskiSpacetime"}
              below.
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
            %p#JetBundles
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundles"} jet bundles
            #JetBundleOfTrivialVectorBundleOverMinkowskiSpacetime.num_defn
              %h6#definition Definition
              %p
                %strong
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
                  of a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+vector+bundle"} trivial vector bundle
                  over
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
              %p
                Given a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+fiber"} field fiber
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+space"} super vector space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
