              %li
                %p
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "#InteractingQantumBVDifferential"} Interacting quantum BV-differential
              %li
                %p
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "#WardIdentities"} Ward identities
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
            %p
              In the
              %a{:href => "#FreeQuantumFields"} previous chapter
              we have found the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
              of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theories"} Lagrangian field theories
              by first choosing a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixed
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+complex"} BV-BRST
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+resolution"} resolution
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariance"} gauge invariant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
              observabes, then applying
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+deformation+quantization"} algebraic deformation quantization
              induced by the resulting
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls-Poisson+bracket"} Peierls-Poisson bracket
              on the graded
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
              to pass to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+algebra"} non-commutative algebra
              of quantum observables, such that, finally, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+differential"} BV-BRST differential
              is respected.
            %p
              Of course most
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
              of interest are non-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"}> free
              ; they are
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+theories"} interacting field theories
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
              is a
              %em non-linear
              differential equation. The archetypical example is the coupling of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
              via the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron-photon+interaction"} electron-photon interaction
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+theory"} interacting field theory
              called
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
              (discussed
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#QuantumElectrodynamics"} below
            %p
              In principle, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
              of such non-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+theories"} interacting field theories
              proceeds the same way: One picks a
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+complex"} BV-BRST
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixing
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls-Poisson+bracket"} Peierls-Poisson bracket
              on the resulting
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
              (
              %a{:href => "Peierls+bracket#Khavkine14"}> Khavkine 14
              ) and then finds a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
              of this
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+structure"} Poisson structure
              to obtain the quantized
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+algebra"} non-commutative algebra
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℏ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            %p
              It turns out (
              %a{:href => "perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory#Collini16"}> Collini 16
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory#HawkinsRejzner16"} Hawkins-Rejzner 16
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InteractingFieldAlgebraOfObservablesIsFormalDeformationQuantization"}
              below) that the resulting
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+theory"} interacting
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
              may equivalently be expressed in terms of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
              (example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ScatteringAmplitudeFromInteractingFieldObservables"}
              below): These are the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitudes"} probability amplitudes
              for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane+waves"} plane waves
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+fields"} free fields
              to come in from the far
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/past"} past
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interact
              in a compact region of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              via the given
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatically switched-off
              outside that region) and to emerge again as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+fields"} free fields
              into the far
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/future"} future
            %p
              The collection of all these
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+vectors"} wave vectors
              of the incoming and outgoing
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+fields"} free fields
              varies, is called the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+S-matrix"} perturbative scattering matrix
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+theory"} interacting field theory
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
              for short. It may equivalently be expressed as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential"} exponential
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatically switched
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
              with itself (def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianFieldTheoryPerturbativeScattering"}
              below). The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorics"} combinatorics
              of the terms in this exponential is captured by
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
              (prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanPerturbationSeriesAwayFromCoincidingPoints"}
              below), which, with some care (remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WorldlineFormalism"}
              below), may be thought of as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+multigraphs"} finite multigraphs
              (def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Graphs"}
              below) whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"} worldlines
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particles"} virtual particles
              and whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
              are the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interactions"} interactions
              that these particles undergo (def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanDiagram"}
              below).
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatic
              definition of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrices"} S-matrices
              for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theories"} Lagrangian field theories
              and their rigorous construction via
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} ("re"-)normalization
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
              (def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExtensionOfTimeOrderedProoductsRenormalization"}
              below) is called
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "causal+perturbation+theory#EpsteinGlaser73"} Epstein-Glaser 73
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+quantization"} path integral quantization
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+theories"} interacting field theories
              (remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InterpretationOfPerturbativeSMatrix"}
              below) and removes the errors (remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheTraditionalErrorThatLeadsToTheNotoriouDivergencies"}
              below) and ensuing puzzlements (expressed in
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson#Feynman85SuchABunchOfWords"} Feynman 85
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
              due to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson"} Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson
              (remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CausalPerturbationTheoryAbsenceOfUVDivergences"}
              below).
            %p
              The equivalent re-formulation of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+theories"} interacting field theories
              in terms of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
              (prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InteractingFieldAlgebraOfObservablesIsFormalDeformationQuantization"}
              below) has the advantage that it gives a direct handle on those
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
              that are measured in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering"} scattering
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
              = succeed "-experiment." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
              – such as notably the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
              – is reflected in such
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
              given via their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal powers
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
              and the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            %p
              Moreover, the mathematical passage from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
              to the actual
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebra"} interacting field algebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+quantum+observables"} of quantum observables
              (def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#QuntumMollerOperator"}
              below) corresponding to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
              is well understood, given via “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bogoliubov%27s+formula"}> Bogoliubov's formula
              ” by the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+M%C3%B8ller+operators"} quantum Møller operators
              (def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InteractingFieldObservables"}
              below).
            %p
              Via
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bogoliubov%27s+formula"} Bogoliubov's formula
              every perturbative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
              scheme (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianFieldTheoryPerturbativeScattering"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatically switched
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
              a notion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+observables"} interacting field observables
              (def.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InteractingFieldObservables"}
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#QuntumMollerOperator"}
              below). By
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bogoliubov%27s+formula"} Bogoliubov's formula
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+switching"} adiabatic switching
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %p
              But this spurious dependence goes away (prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#IsomorphismFromChangeOfAdiabaticSwitching"}
              below) when restricting attention to observables whose spacetime support is inside a compact
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causally+closed+subsets"} causally closed subsets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
              of spacetime (def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PerturbativeGeneratingLocalNetOfObservables"}
              below). This is a sensible condition for an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
              nessecarily probes only a compact subset of spacetime, see also remark
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdiabaticLimit"}
            %p
              The resulting system (a “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-presheaf"}> co-presheaf
              ”) of well-defined perturbative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebras+of+observables"} interacting field algebras of observables
              (def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SystemOfAlgebrasOfQuantumObservables"}
              below)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi IntObs
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi L
                      %mi int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{O} \mapsto IntObs(E_{\text{BV-BRST}}, \mathbf{L}_{int})(\mathcal{O})
            %p
              is in fact
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causally local
              (prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PerturbativeQuantumObservablesIsLocalnet"}
              below). This fact was presupposed without proof already in
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory#IlinSlavnov78"} Il’in-Slavnov 78
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
              (
              %a{:href => "Haag-Kastler+axioms#HaagKastler64"}> Haag-Kastler 64
              ) demand of an intrinsically defined
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              (i.e. defined without necessarily making recourse to the geometric backdrop of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
              is that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
              be
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
              and hence is necessarily violated in the present context of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
              is commonly known as
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causally+local+nets+of+observables"} causally local nets of observables
              but without the
              = succeed "-condition" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
              (
              %a{:href => "perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory#DuetschFredenhagen01"}> Dütsch-Fredenhagen 01
              ,
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory#FredenhagenRejzner12"} Fredenhagen-Rejzner 12
            %p
              In this terminology the content of prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PerturbativeQuantumObservablesIsLocalnet"}
              below is that
              = succeed ":" do
                %em
                  while the input of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixed
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
