            #TerminologyForEffectiveAction.num_remark
              %h6#remark_18 Remark
              %p
                %strong (terminology for “effective action”)
              %p Beware differing conventions of terminology:
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    In the perspective of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
                    (remark
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#pQFTEffective"}
                    below), the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+action"} effective action
                    in def.
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InPerturbationTheoryActionEffective"}
                    is sometimes called the
                    %em effective potential
                    at scale
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Λ
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda = 0
                    (see prop.
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InPerturbationTheoryActionEffective"}
                    below).
                  %p
                    This terminology originates in restriction to the special example of the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
                    (example
                    = succeed ")," do
                      %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RealScalarFieldBundle"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phi%5En+interactions"} Phi^n interactions
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mi int
                          %mo =
                          %munder
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                            %mi n
                          %munder
                            %mo ∫
                            %mi Σ
                          %msubsup
                            %mi g
                            %mi sw
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi n
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Φ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi n
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %msub
                            %mi dvol
                            %mi Σ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g S_{int} = \underset{n}{\sum} \underset{\Sigma}{\int} g_{sw}^{(n)}(x) (\mathbf{\Phi}(x))^n \, dvol_\Sigma(x)
                    (example
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#phintheoryLagrangian"}
                    = succeed "-terms." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/potential+energy"} potential energy
                  %p
                    From this perspective the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+action"} effective action
                    in def.
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InPerturbationTheoryActionEffective"}
                    is a special case of
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+effective+actions"} relative effective actions
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mrow
                              %mi eff
                              %mo ,
                              %mi Λ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{eff,\Lambda}
                    (“relative effective potentials”, in the case of
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phi%5En+interactions"} Phi^n interactions
                    = succeed "-scales" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+cutoff"} UV cutoff
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Λ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
                    (def.
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EffectiveActionRelative"}
                    below).
                %li
                  %p For the special case that
                  .maruku-equation
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi j
                          %mi A
                          %mo ≔
                          %munder
                            %mo ∫
                            %mi Σ
                          %msub
                            %mi j
                            %mrow
                              %mi sw
                              %mo ,
                              %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi Φ
                            %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %msub
                            %mi dvol
                            %mi Σ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                          j A
                          \\coloneqq
                          \\underset{\Sigma}{\int} j_{sw,a}(x) \mathbf{\Phi}^a(x)\, dvol_{\Sigma}(x)
                  %p
                    is a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polynomial+observable"} regular
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+observable"} linear observable
                    (def.
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RegularLinearFieldObservables"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+action"} effective action
                    according to def.
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InPerturbationTheoryActionEffective"}
                    is often denoted
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi W
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W(j)
                    or
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi E
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(j)
                    %em
                      functional
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Legendre+transform"} Legendre transform
                    (if that makes sense) is instead called the effective action, instead.
                  %p
                    This is because the latter encodes the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
                    for the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+values"} vacuum expectation values
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Φ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi int
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \mathbf{\Phi}(x)_int\rangle
                    of the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+observables"} interacting
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+observables"} field observables
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EquationsOfMotionForVacuumExpectationValues"}
                    below.
              %p
                Notice the different meaning of “effective” in both cases: In the first case it refers to what is effectively seen of the full
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pQFT"} pQFT
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    at some
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-cutoff+scale"} UV-cutoff scale
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+values"} vacuum expectation values
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polynomial+observable"} regular
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+observables"} linear observables
            #LogarithmEffectiveAction.num_prop
              %h6#proposition_16 Proposition
              %p
                %strong
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+action"} effective action
                  is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logarithm"} logarithm
                  of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
              %p
                Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mtext BV-BRST
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi L
                      %mo ′
