              %p
                This is the way in which the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
                is used in practice for computing
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
            %p#InteractingQantumBVDifferential
              %strong Interacting quantum BV-Differential
            %p
              So far we have discussed, starting with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free field
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebras+of+observables"} interacting field algebras of observables
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#QuntumMollerOperator"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+order"}> loop order
              , prop.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanDiagramLoopOrder"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
              (example
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanPerturbationSeries"}
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SMatrixVacuumContribution"}
            %p
              But this
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebra+of+observables"} interacting field algebra of observables
              still involves all the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/auxiliary+fields"} auxiliary fields
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free field
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
              (example
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldSpeciesQED"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariance"} gauge invariant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
              observables should be (just) the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+differential"} BV-BRST differential
              on this enlarged space of observables. Hence for the construction of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
              to conclude, it remains to pass the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+differential"} BV-BRST differential
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free field
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
              of observables to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
              on the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebra"} interacting field algebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
              is well defined.
            %p
              Since the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
              away from coinciding interaction points and as well as on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polynomial+observables"} regular polynomial observables
              are uniquely fixed (prop.
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TimeOrderedProductAwayFromDiagonal"}
              %em interacting quantum BV-differential
              is uniquely fixed, on
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polynomial+observables"} regular polynomial observables
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugation"} conjugation
              with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+M%C3%B8ller+operators"} quantum Møller operators
              (def.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BVDifferentialInteractingQuantum"}
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+master+equation"} quantum master equation
              or equivalently the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+master+Ward+identity"} quantum master Ward identity
              (prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#QuantumMasterEquation"}
              below).
            %p
              When
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extending
              to coinciding interaction points via
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} ("re"-)normalization
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExtensionOfTimeOrderedProoductsRenormalization"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+conditions"} renormalization conditions
              (def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RenormalizationConditions"}
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BasicConditionsRenormalization"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/master+Ward+identity"} master Ward identity
              then imply corrections to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noether%27s+theorem"} Noether current
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conserved+current"} conservation laws
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#WardIdentities"} below
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
            %p
              For the following discussion, recall from the
              %a{:href => "#FreeQuantumFields"} previous chapter
              how the global BV-differential
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi S
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi PolyObs
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi reg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi PolyObs
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi reg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\{S',-\}
                    \\;\colon\;
                    PolyObs(E_{\text{BV-BRST}})_{reg}[ [\hbar] ]
                    \\longrightarrow
                    PolyObs(E_{\text{BV-BRST}})_{reg}[ [\hbar] ]
            %p
              on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polynomial+observables"} regular polynomial observables
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BVDifferentialGlobal"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antibracket"} antibracket
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{-,-\}
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ComplexBVBRSTGlobal"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugation"} conjugated
              into the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
              via the time ordering operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi S
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ∘
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T} \circ \{-S',-\} \circ \mathcal{T}^{-}
              (def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AntibracketTimeOrdered"}
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GaugeFixedActionFunctionalTimeOrderedAntibracket"}
            %p
              In the same way we may use the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+M%C3%B8ller+operators"} quantum Møller operators
              to conjugate the BV-differential into the regular part of the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+field+algebra+of+observables"} interacting field algebra of observables
            #BVDifferentialInteractingQuantum.num_defn
              %h6#definition_25 Definition
              %p
                %strong
                  (interacting quantum
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-differential"} BV-differential
              %p
                Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mtext BV-BRST
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi L
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E_{\text{BV-BRST}}, \mathbf{L}', \Delta_H )
                be a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
                according to def.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#VacuumFree"}
                and let
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
